us should be 'Canceled' after filtering)\n            if status.lower() == \"canceled\":\n                cancellation_counts[customer_name] = cancellation_counts.get(customer_name, 0) + 1\n    \n    # Check if any cancellations were found\n    if not cancellation_counts:\n        return \"No customers with cancellations found.\"\n    \n    # Find the customer with the most cancellations\n    max_customer = max(cancellation_counts.items(), key=lambda item: item[1])[0]\n    \n    # Filter orders for this customer with status 'canceled'\n    customer_cancelled_orders = [\n        order for order in order_data \n        if order[\"customer_name\"] == max_customer and order[\"status\"].lower() == \"canceled\"\n    ]\n    \n    if not customer_cancelled_orders:\n        return f\"No cancelled orders found for customer {max_customer}.\"\n    \n    # Sort by purchase date descending to get most recent\n    # Assuming purchase_date is in a sortable format like 'May 31, 2023 2:55:09 AM'\n    customer_cancelled_orders.sort(key=lambda x: x[\"purchase_date\"], reverse=True)\n    \n    # Get the most recent cancelled order\n    most_recent_order = customer_cancelled_orders[0]\n    total_spend = most_recent_order[\"grand_total\"]\n    \n    return total_spend",
